#include <memory>

class SmartPtr {
public:
  // Constructor
  SmartPtr(T* p) : ptr_(p) {}

  // Dtor
  ~SmartPtr() {
    if (ptr_) delete ptr_;
  }

  // Accessor
  T* get() const { return ptr_; }

  // Mutator
  void set(T* p) { ptr_ = p; }

private:
  // Data member
  std::unique_ptr<T> ptr_;
};
```
This class provides a smart pointer that wraps a raw pointer to an object of type `T`. The smart pointer has getters and setters for the raw pointer, and also has a destructor that deletes the object if it is no longer being used.

To use the smart pointer, you can create a new instance and then use the `get()` and `set()` methods to access and modify the underlying object:
```
T* p = new T;

SmartPtr sp(p);

// Access the object through the smart pointer
T* obj = sp.get();

// Modify the object through the smart pointer
obj = new T;
sp.set(obj);
```
This is just one example of a smart pointer class. There are many other ways to implement smart pointers in C++, and the best approach will depend on your specific requirements.

Here's an example of a non-recursive Python function that tests if an integer is prime or not:

```
def is_prime(num):
    if num < 2:
        return False
    for i in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):
        if num % i == 0:
            return False
    return True
```

This function uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes to generate a list of prime numbers up to the square root of the input integer, and then checks if the input integer is divisible by any of those primes. It returns `` True `` if the input integer is prime, and `` False `` otherwise.

